  <h2>Decisions designed to compound</h2>
  <ol class="decision-grid">
    <li><span class="decision-number">01</span><h3>Technology and architecture</h3><p>Selected a React and TypeScript component model, Vite-based tooling and a Sass architecture capable of serving both React and non-React products.</p></li>
    <li><span class="decision-number">02</span><h3>Tokens into interface</h3><p>Defined the boundary between Vetsource design tokens, component styles and public APIs so visual decisions could remain consistent in implementation.</p></li>
    <li><span class="decision-number">03</span><h3>Documentation as product</h3><p>Made Storybook the shared surface for behavior, usage guidance, accessibility expectations and collaboration between design and engineering.</p></li>
    <li><span class="decision-number">04</span><h3>Repeatable delivery</h3><p>Established the package, versioning and publishing foundations that allowed product teams to adopt improvements incrementally.</p></li>
  </ol>

  <h2>A mature base by the 2.8 release</h2>
  <div class="milestone-panel">
    <div>
      <span class="milestone-label">Approximate final milestone</span>
      <strong class="milestone-version">2.8.0</strong>
      <span class="milestone-date">March 5, 2024</span>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>By this release, Kibble had grown beyond its original primitives into a broader system with reusable components, utilities and a documented contribution model.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Steps, ToastProvider and reusable hooks joined the library.</li>
        <li>Component APIs and shared media-query utilities continued to mature.</li>
        <li>Accessibility and long-content behavior were improved across components.</li>
        <li>The changelog made adoption and breaking changes visible to consumers.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>A foundation the team could keep extending</h2>
  <p>Today Kibble is presented as Vetsource’s global design system: a custom system built from scratch, supporting reusable UI for React and non-React applications, patterns and brand guidance. Its current breadth reflects the work of the Vetsource design-system team after my time on the project as well as the foundations I helped establish.</p>
  <p>The result I value most is not a single component. It is that the architecture, documentation model and delivery workflow were durable enough for other people to continue evolving the system.</p>

  <p class="case-note"><strong>Scope note:</strong> This case study describes my contribution from before implementation began through approximately the 2.8.0 release. The live Storybook includes substantial later work by the Vetsource team.</p>

